l voice is very  much poetic  um there were moments where i was scared  that it would it was too poetic and i  tried to you know strip the poetry out  and my agent sarah burns she was just  really upset  she was like what what did you do yeah  um  and so but in terms of calling it love  songs  it's uh it's a love letter to black  women yeah and again  black women deep brown  black women coily haired kinky haired um  the kind of women that i view as  beautiful  you know um women that look like um  oprah women that look like michelle  obama women that look like viola davis  um you know i wanted again to have a  different story a story that would  celebrate us  in in all our beauty and complexity  honorary i'm i'm fascinated by your  process and i'm fascinated by how these  voices came to you and your dreams  what did you  what did you make of them when you first  started hearing them  um i dream  uh  poems a lot  poetry is like prayer for me  and so  um i'm used to waking up with language  um you know in my spirit in my head and  writing them down  the hardest part was taking what at  first i thought were just long prose  poems  and then making them into a story that's  what took a lot longer  um if i was just going to write a series  of poems  yeah i would have been done with that in  you know 18 months two years but trying  to make sure that these characters were  fully developed and then connecting them  to ailey so that everything comes  together at the end of the book that's  what took a long time  professor jefferson let me just say this  the atlantic says with the love songs of  w.e.b du bois  de voice jeffers has created an opus an  indelible entry into the canon of  contemporary american literature one of  the foundational fictional tests of  black literature worthy of sitting  alongside tony morrison's the bluest eye  ralph ellison's invisible man and  jasmine ward's sing unburied saying  that's a pretty good company and i  understand when oprah called you y